@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Demonstrates async (open arrowhead) vs sync (filled arrowhead) messages, reply (dashed), and error-handling patterns using alt and break fragments
'   usecase:      Educational example showing how message arrow styles encode the sender's blocking behaviour and how fragments model error paths
'   complexity:   medium
'   domain:       Integration
' ============================================================================
title Example Sequence Diagram - Async vs Sync and Error Handling

' ============================================================================
' CONCEPTS TAUGHT
'   - ? (async, open arrowhead)   : Sender -> Receiver : message
'                        PlantUML draws an OPEN arrowhead when the message
'                        is marked asynchronous; the caller does NOT block.
'   - -> (sync, filled arrowhead) : the caller blocks until the receiver
'                        returns; paired with a dashed reply
'   - --> (reply / return)        : dashed arrow carrying a return value
'   - alt / else / end            : alternatives branch on a guard
'   - break                       : an exception-handling fragment; if its
'                        guard fires, the body executes and the surrounding
'                        interaction is ABORTED (breaks out of any loop)
'   - note                        : free-text annotation anchored to a
'                        participant or message for teaching readers
' ============================================================================

' Four participants spanning a client, an API gateway, and two workers.
actor "Caller" as CL
participant "API Gateway" as GW
queue "Message Bus" as MB #LightCoral
participant "Worker" as WK

' --- ASYNC fire-and-forget section --------------------------------------
' The Caller hands work to the Gateway and immediately continues; the arrow
' carries an open (asynchronous) arrowhead because no reply is expected.
CL -> GW : submitJob(payload)
activate GW
GW -> MB : publish(jobId)
' The publish is itself async - GW does not wait for the worker to ack.
note right of GW
  Async: open arrowhead,
  caller does not block.
end note
GW --> CL : accepted(jobId)
deactivate GW

' --- SYNSYNC request/reply section -------------------------------------
' MB hands the job to a Worker. The Worker then makes a SYNCHRONOUS call to
' fetch configuration - it cannot proceed until the reply arrives.
MB -> WK : deliver(jobId)
activate WK
WK -> GW : fetchConfig(jobId)
activate GW
GW --> WK : config
deactivate GW

' --- ERROR-HANDLING alternatives ---------------------------------------
alt [config valid]
  ' Happy path: process and acknowledge.
  WK -> MB : ack(jobId)
  WK --> CL : done(result)
else [config invalid]
  ' Second branch: surface the error and bail out.
  WK -> MB : deadLetter(jobId)
  WK --> CL : failed(error)
end

' BREAK FRAGMENT: model an unrecoverable fault that aborts the surrounding
' interaction entirely. If the guard is true, the break body runs and no
' further messages in the enclosing scope are sent.
break [worker crashed]
  MB -> GW : requeue(jobId)
  activate GW
  GW --> CL : retried(jobId)
  deactivate GW
end

deactivate WK

@enduml
